{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And all the wise-hearted did.</b> Meaning: even though those who did the work were many, nevertheless the work was done as though one man had made it. And the intention in this is that when many perform a work, the form of one man's craft will inevitably differ from another's, and it will not resemble a work that a single craftsman makes in its entirety. And Scripture informs us that the work of the Mishkan was complete also in this respect, for He who gives wisdom to the beast equaled them in the contemplation of wisdom — all alike — and they were all like one person. And according to this approach, the reason it uses singular language for every detail of the works is resolved, without specifying who it is — and it is obvious that it does not refer back to Betzalel, for he did not do everything, as the trustworthy witness states (Exodus 37:1): ״וַיַּעַשׂ בְצַלְאֵל אֶת הָאָרוֹן״ — rather it intends the makers of the work, and it mentions them in singular language for the reason I have written." "<b>Wise-hearted among the doers, etc.</b> Perhaps it alludes that through the mediation of their making the work, and through its cause, they became wise-hearted." "<b>And they made, etc.</b> The reason that Scripture repeated all the details and did not suffice with saying ״וַיַּעַשׂ כַּאֲשֶׁר צִוָּה ה׳ אֶת מֹשֶׁה״ is along the lines of what they said, of blessed memory (Genesis Rabbah 60), regarding the double narration of the account of Eliezer servant of Abraham — because it was beloved to Him, He wrote it twice. So too Scripture revealed regarding the matter of the making of the Mishkan that it was beloved to Him, and therefore it was written twice."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 36, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:37:07.303469143Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}